ic - deep-copied storage snapshot before batch; on any error restore default table data, reset table._next_id, clear_cache; missing remove id is an error; previously acquired handles reflect success and rollback.\nR03: ordinary APIs unchanged; deep-copy on insert (no aliasing either way).\n\nR04: bool doc_id rejected (isinstance bool check first); structural validation of the whole batch before any commit; non-list operations and non-dict documents raise ValueError; empty dict documents accepted.\nR05: preview(ops) simulates on a deep-copied table snapshot read from storage; never writes, never consumes IDs (reads table._next_id without mutating; falls back to max(id)+1 like Table._get_next_id); errors leave data and next-ID unchanged; existing handles and query caches untouched (preview clears no cache). Storage keys are normalised from str to int (JSON storage).\nR05b: preview and apply return fresh detached lists; mutating a returned list has no effect on later calls or writer state.\nR05 clarification: successful apply advances the table _next_id counter (persisted via storage writes through _update_table); failed apply restores the counter, so IDs are never consumed by failures; subsequent inserts via apply or direct db.insert continue the sequence without reuse/rewind, even across removes.\nR06: no stale snapshot - every call reads current storage; direct db.insert between calls is seen by apply and preview.\n\nCONFLICT RESOLUTION: an older generated note claimed \"preview may consume IDs and bool IDs are accepted\". That note is unverified and superseded: preview NEVER consumes IDs (tested: test_preview_does_not_consume_ids, test_preview_on_error_consumes_nothing) and bool doc_ids are REJECTED (test_bool_doc_id_rejected). The happy-path smoke does not cover these boundaries; the tests above do.\n\nTests: tests/test_journal.py (123 tests, memory + JSON storage). Full suite tests/ + acceptance_public.py: 350 passed.\nRun pytest with -o addopts= (pytest.ini adds coverage flags not installed here).\nNote: use tinydb.where(...) for queries, not Query(...).\n---\n__init__.py\ndatabase.py\njournal.py\nmiddlewares.py\nmypy_plugin.py\noperations.py\npy.typed\nqueries.py\nstorages.py\ntable.py\nutils.py\nversion.py\n---\nimport pytest\nfrom tinydb import TinyDB, Query\nfrom tinydb.storages import MemoryStorage\nfrom tinydb.journal import BatchWriter\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef pair():\n    db = TinyDB(storage=MemoryStorage)\n    return (db, BatchWriter(db))\n\ndef test_public_basic(pair):\n    db, w = pair\n    assert w.apply([{'op': 'insert', 'document': {'x': 1}}]) == [1]\n    assert db.all()[0]['x'] == 1\n    assert w.apply([{'op': 'remove', 'doc_id': 1}]) == []\n    assert len(db) == 0\n\ndef test_public_atomic(pair):\n    db, w = pair\n    db.insert({'x': 1})\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n        w.apply([{'op': 'insert', 'document': {'x': 2}}, {'op': 'remove', 'doc_id': 99}])\n    assert [v['x'] for v in db.all()] == [1]\n\ndef test_public_preview(pair):\n    db, w = pair\n    ops = [{'op': 'insert', 'document': {}}]\n    assert w.preview(ops) == [1] and len(db) == 0\n    assert w.apply(ops) == [1]\n\ndef test_public_idempotent(pair):\n    db, w = pair\n    ops = [{'op': 'insert', 'document': {'x': 1}}]\n    assert w.apply(ops, token='a') == [1]\n    assert w.apply(ops, token='a') == [1]\n    assert len(db) == 1\nimport pytest"}